| | | | |  The following code snippet demonstrates how to create a simple HTTP server using Node.js and Express.js:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/hello', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello World!');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

This code creates an HTTP server that listens on port 3000 and responds with "Hello World!" when a GET request is made to the `/hello` endpoint.

To test this server, you can use `curl` or any HTTP client:

```sh
curl http://localhost:3000/hello
```

This will output:

```
Hello World!
```

Feel free to modify the `app.get` route to handle other HTTP methods (like `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) and different endpoints to build a more complex application.
